\subsection{Energy balance in a series loop}

Charge that goes once around a closed loop must finish with the same energy it started with.
So the energy \emph{gained} per coulomb in the cell equals the energy \emph{spent} per coulomb in all
the loads:

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.05, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l={$\mathcal{E}$}] (0,2.5)
        to[R, l={$R_1$}, a={$V_1$}] (3.0,2.5)
        to[R, l_={$R_2$}, a^={$V_2$}] (3.0,0)
        -- (0,0);
\end{circuitikz}
\quad
\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline=(current bounding box.center), scale=0.9]
  \node[align=left, font=\small] at (0,1.2) {%
    per coulomb:\\[0.35em]
    gain in cell: $\mathcal{E}$\\[0.2em]
    spend in $R_1$: $V_1$\\[0.2em]
    spend in $R_2$: $V_2$\\[0.35em]
    $\Rightarrow\ \mathcal{E}=V_1+V_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

Energy transferred when charge $Q$ moves through a p.d.\ $V$ (or e.m.f.\ $\mathcal{E}$):
\[
E = VQ \qquad\text{or}\qquad E = \mathcal{E}Q.
\]
So if $Q$ goes once around the loop: cell supplies $\mathcal{E}Q$; resistor $R_1$ converts $V_1 Q$;
resistor $R_2$ converts $V_2 Q$; and $\mathcal{E}Q = V_1 Q + V_2 Q$.

