\subsection{Potential along a path (``hill diagram'')}

In circuit questions, a ``hill diagram'' of height usually means the \textbf{electric potential}
(or PE per coulomb) along the wire. Electrons lose PE when they go through a resistor in the
direction of the conventional drop.

\ex
An electron moves through a network of identical resistors from terminal $P$ to $O$ to $N$ to $M$.
Where is its energy change the \emph{greatest}?
\sol[2.5cm]{%
Greatest $|\Delta V|$ along the path. Compare potential drops between consecutive points; the
largest p.d.\ step gives the greatest energy change per electron ($E=e\Delta V$).
}

