\ex
A $12\,\mathrm{V}$ cell feeds the network in Figure~(a).
An ideal wire is then connected between $A$ and $B$ as in Figure~(b).
How does the p.d.\ across the top-right $4\,\Omega$ resistor change?
\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=0.95, transform shape]
  \node[font=\small\bfseries] at (2.6,3.55) {(a) original};
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l_=$12\,\mathrm{V}$] (0,2.8);
  \draw (0,2.8) to[R, l=$2\,\Omega$] (2.2,2.8) node[circ]{} node[above,font=\scriptsize] {$A$}
        to[R, l=$4\,\Omega$] (5.0,2.8) -- (5.0,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw (0,0) to[R, l_=$4\,\Omega$] (2.2,0) node[circ]{} node[below,font=\scriptsize] {$B$}
        to[R, l_=$2\,\Omega$] (5.0,0);
\end{circuitikz}
\qquad
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=0.95, transform shape]
  \node[font=\small\bfseries] at (2.6,3.55) {(b) wire added};
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1, l_=$12\,\mathrm{V}$] (0,2.8);
  \draw (0,2.8) to[R, l=$2\,\Omega$] (2.2,2.8) node[circ]{} node[above,font=\scriptsize] {$A$}
        to[R, l=$4\,\Omega$] (5.0,2.8) -- (5.0,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw (0,0) to[R, l_=$4\,\Omega$] (2.2,0) node[circ]{} node[below,font=\scriptsize] {$B$}
        to[R, l_=$2\,\Omega$] (5.0,0);
  \draw[thick] (2.2,2.8) -- (2.2,0);
  \node[font=\scriptsize,right] at (2.35,1.4) {new wire};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}
\sol[4.0cm]{%
\textbf{(a)} Each column is $6\,\Omega$; both columns share the full $12\,\mathrm{V}$, so
$I=2\,\mathrm{A}$ in each branch.
Along the top branch: $V_A=12-2(2)=8\,\mathrm{V}$; the top-right $4\,\Omega$ has the full
drop from $A$ to the right rail $\Rightarrow$ $V_{4\,\Omega}=8\,\mathrm{V}$.
Along the bottom branch: $V_B=12-2(4)=4\,\mathrm{V}$, so $V_{AB}=4\,\mathrm{V}$ ($A$ and $B$ are
\emph{not} equipotential).

\textbf{(b)} The new wire joins $A$ and $B$ $\Rightarrow$ same colour $\Rightarrow$ one potential
$V_A=V_B=6\,\mathrm{V}$ (the two branch currents split so the mid-points meet at half the supply).
The top-right $4\,\Omega$ now runs from $6\,\mathrm{V}$ to $0\,\mathrm{V}$
$\Rightarrow$ $V_{4\,\Omega}=6\,\mathrm{V}$.

Change: $8\,\mathrm{V}\to 6\,\mathrm{V}$ (decreases by $2\,\mathrm{V}$).
}

