\subsection{How to connect an ammeter}

An \textbf{ammeter} measures the current passing \emph{through} a part of the circuit.
Wire it \textbf{in series}: break the path and insert the meter so every coulomb that passes
through that branch also passes through the ammeter.

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.0, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1] (0,2.0)
        to[ammeter, l=A] (2.0,2.0)
        to[R, l=$R$] (4.0,2.0)
        -- (4.0,0) -- (0,0);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.0,-0.55) {in series with $R$};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em,itemsep=0.12em,topsep=0.25em]
  \item Ideal ammeter: $R\approx 0$, so it does not noticeably change the current.
  \item Match polarity: connect the meter's $+$ terminal toward the battery $+$ side of the loop.
  \item To measure current \emph{through one branch} only, put A in that branch alone---not in the
        main line before a parallel split.
\end{itemize}

