\subsection{How to connect a voltmeter}

A \textbf{voltmeter} measures the p.d.\ \emph{between} two points.
Wire it \textbf{in parallel}: connect one lead to each point, without breaking the main circuit.

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.0, transform shape]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1] (0,2.0)
        to[R, l=$R$] (3.6,2.0)
        -- (3.6,0) -- (0,0);
  \draw (0,2.0) to[voltmeter, l=V] (3.6,2.0);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.8,-0.55) {in parallel with $R$};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}

\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.2em,itemsep=0.12em,topsep=0.25em]
  \item Ideal voltmeter: $R\approx\infty$, so it draws almost no current and does not change the
        p.d.\ being measured.
  \item Match polarity: the meter's $+$ lead goes to the point at higher potential.
  \item The reading is path-independent: it is always $V_A-V_B$ between the two probe points,
        no matter how charge could travel from $A$ to $B$ elsewhere in the network.
\end{itemize}

