\ex
In the network below, three paths connect $A$ to $B$.
When $1\,\mathrm{C}$ of charge moves from $A$ to $B$ along path~1, it loses $2\,\mathrm{J}$.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\alph*),itemsep=0.05em,topsep=0.25em,leftmargin=1.6em]
  \item What is $V_{AB}$ along path~1?
  \item What is $V_{AB}$ if the charge takes path~2 or path~3 instead?
  \item Explain why the p.d.\ between two points does not depend on the route taken.
\end{enumerate}
\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[scale=1.0, transform shape]
  \draw (0,3.3) to[battery1] (0,2.2);
  \node[circ] at (0,2.2) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,left] at (-0.1,2.2) {$A$};
  \draw (0,3.3) -- (4.4,3.3);
  \node[circ] at (2.2,3.3) {};
  \node[font=\scriptsize,above] at (2.2,3.48) {$B$};
  \draw (0,2.2) -- (2.2,2.2);
  \draw (2.2,2.2) to[R, l=\scriptsize $2\,\mathrm{V}$] (2.2,3.3);
  \draw (2.2,2.2) to[R] (3.3,2.2) to[R] (4.4,2.2);
  \draw (2.2,2.2) to[R, l=\scriptsize $1\,\mathrm{V}$] (2.2,1.1);
  \draw (2.2,1.1) to[R, l=\scriptsize $1\,\mathrm{V}$] (4.4,1.1);
  \draw (4.4,1.1) -- (4.4,3.3);
  \draw (0,2.2) to[R, l=\scriptsize $1\,\mathrm{V}$] (0,1.1);
  \draw (0,1.1) to[R, l=\scriptsize $1\,\mathrm{V}$] (2.2,1.1);
  \draw (2.2,1.1) to[R] (3.3,1.1);
  \draw (3.3,1.1) to[R, l=\scriptsize $\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{V}$] (3.3,2.2)
        to[R, l=\scriptsize $\tfrac{1}{2}\,\mathrm{V}$] (3.3,3.3);
  \draw (3.3,3.3) -- (2.2,3.3);
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.0,2.55) {%
    \tikz{\node[circle,draw,inner sep=0.8pt,font=\scriptsize]{1};}};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (3.2,0.85) {%
    \tikz{\node[circle,draw,inner sep=0.8pt,font=\scriptsize]{2};}};
  \node[font=\scriptsize] at (1.5,0.75) {%
    \tikz{\node[circle,draw,inner sep=0.8pt,font=\scriptsize]{3};}};
\end{circuitikz}
\end{center}
\sol[4.5cm]{%
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Energy loss $2\,\mathrm{J}$ per coulomb $\Rightarrow$ $V_{AB}=2\,\mathrm{V}$.
  \item Still $2\,\mathrm{V}$ along path~2 ($1+1\,\mathrm{V}$) and along path~3 as well.
        The route changes, but the endpoints do not.
  \item Every point in the circuit has one definite potential (once the earthed point is fixed).
        $V_{AB}=V_A-V_B$ depends only on those two values, not on which path joins them.
\end{enumerate}
}

