%======================================================================
\section{Resistance and Ohm's law}

\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}[font=\small, >=Latex]
  \node[align=center] (V) at (0,0) {p.d.\ $V$\\[0.12em]{\scriptsize (external drive)}};
  \draw[thick] ($(V.east)+(0.08,0.12)$) -- (2.35,0.12);
  \draw[thick] ($(V.east)+(0.08,-0.12)$) -- (2.35,-0.12);
  \draw[-{Latex}, thick] (2.35,0.12) -- (2.85,0.12);
  \draw[-{Latex}, thick] (2.35,-0.12) -- (2.85,-0.12);
  \node[draw, thick, minimum width=2.7cm, minimum height=1.05cm, align=center] (Rbox) at (4.15,0)
    {resistance $R$};
  \node[font=\scriptsize, below=0.38cm of Rbox] {(property of the load)};
  \draw[thick] (5.45,0.12) -- (7.72,0.12);
  \draw[thick] (5.45,-0.12) -- (7.72,-0.12);
  \draw[-{Latex}, thick] (7.72,0.12) -- (8.22,0.12);
  \draw[-{Latex}, thick] (7.72,-0.12) -- (8.22,-0.12);
  \node[align=center] at (9.15,0) {current $I$\\[0.12em]{\scriptsize (response)}};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

A p.d.\ $V$ across a load (the \emph{external drive}) produces a current $I$ (the \emph{response}).
How large $I$ is for a given $V$ depends on the load's resistance $R$---a property of the component
itself.

Resistance is defined as
\begin{center}
\begin{tikzpicture}
  \node[draw=black!25, line width=0.4pt, fill=yellow!22,
        inner xsep=16pt, inner ysep=8pt] {$\displaystyle R=\frac{V}{I}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
\end{center}

The SI unit of resistance is the \textbf{ohm} ($\Omega$).
A load has a resistance of $1\,\Omega$ if it draws a current of $1\,\mathrm{A}$ when a p.d.\
of $1\,\mathrm{V}$ is applied across it:
\[
1\,\Omega = 1\,\mathrm{V\,A^{-1}}.
\]
Rearranging gives $V=IR$ and $I=V/R$; at fixed $V$, larger $R$ $\Rightarrow$ smaller $I$.

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{@{}p{0.28\textwidth}p{0.66\textwidth}@{}}
\toprule
Idea & \\
\midrule
Resistance
  & $R=V/I$ at an operating point; larger $R$ $\Rightarrow$ harder to drive current. \\
Ohm's law

Rearranging gives $V=IR$ and $I=V/R$; at fixed $V$, larger $R$ $\Rightarrow$ smaller $I$.

